### What are the core entities?

The core entities are `Vehicle`, `ParkingSpot`, `ParkingFloor`, `ParkingLot`, `Ticket`, and `FeeStrategy`. Each has a single responsibility.

```java
package com.bank.parking.model;

public enum VehicleType {
    BIKE, CAR, TRUCK
}
```

```java
package com.bank.parking.model;

public class Vehicle {
    private final String licensePlate;
    private final VehicleType type;

    public Vehicle(String licensePlate, VehicleType type) {
        this.licensePlate = licensePlate;
        this.type = type;
    }

    public String getLicensePlate() { return licensePlate; }
    public VehicleType getType() { return type; }
}
```

### How does ParkingSpot work?

A `ParkingSpot` has a size, a spot number, and an occupied flag. It owns its own state transitions: the spot decides how it becomes occupied or freed.

```java
package com.bank.parking.model;

public class ParkingSpot {
    private final String spotId;
    private final VehicleType size;
    private boolean occupied;

    public ParkingSpot(String spotId, VehicleType size) {
        this.spotId = spotId;
        this.size = size;
        this.occupied = false;
    }

    public boolean canFit(VehicleType vehicleType) {
        return this.size.ordinal() >= vehicleType.ordinal();
    }

    public synchronized void occupy() {
        if (occupied) throw new IllegalStateException(
                "Spot " + spotId + " is already occupied");
        occupied = true;
    }

    public synchronized void free() {
        if (!occupied) throw new IllegalStateException(
                "Spot " + spotId + " is already free");
        occupied = false;
    }

    public boolean isOccupied() { return occupied; }
    public String getSpotId() { return spotId; }
    public VehicleType getSize() { return size; }
}
```

### How does ParkingFloor find the nearest spot?

A `ParkingFloor` holds a list of spots and provides a method to find the nearest free spot that can fit a given vehicle type.

```java
package com.bank.parking.model;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class ParkingFloor {
    private final String floorId;
    private final List<ParkingSpot> spots;

    public ParkingFloor(String floorId, List<ParkingSpot> spots) {
        this.floorId = floorId;
        this.spots = spots;
    }

    public Optional<ParkingSpot> findNearestSpot(VehicleType type) {
        return spots.stream()
                .filter(s -> !s.isOccupied() && s.canFit(type))
                .findFirst();
    }

    public String getFloorId() { return floorId; }
}
```

For production scale, replace the linear scan with a per-size priority queue keyed by distance so lookups are logarithmic.

### How does the ticket system work?

A `Ticket` records the entry time, the assigned spot, and the vehicle reference.

```java
package com.bank.parking.model;

import java.time.LocalDateTime;

public class Ticket {
    private final String ticketId;
    private final Vehicle vehicle;
    private final ParkingSpot spot;
    private final LocalDateTime entryTime;

    public Ticket(String ticketId, Vehicle vehicle,
                  ParkingSpot spot, LocalDateTime entryTime) {
        this.ticketId = ticketId;
        this.vehicle = vehicle;
        this.spot = spot;
        this.entryTime = entryTime;
    }

    public String getTicketId() { return ticketId; }
    public Vehicle getVehicle() { return vehicle; }
    public ParkingSpot getSpot() { return spot; }
    public LocalDateTime getEntryTime() { return entryTime; }
}
```

### How does payment work with a FeeStrategy?

A `FeeStrategy` interface lets the lot support different pricing models without editing the lot code.

```java
package com.bank.parking.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface FeeStrategy {
    BigDecimal computeFee(long hoursParked);
}
```

```java
package com.bank.parking.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class HourlyFeeStrategy implements FeeStrategy {
    private final BigDecimal hourlyRate;

    public HourlyFeeStrategy(BigDecimal hourlyRate) {
        this.hourlyRate = hourlyRate;
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal computeFee(long hoursParked) {
        return hourlyRate.multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(
                Math.max(1, hoursParked)));
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.parking.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class FlatFeeStrategy implements FeeStrategy {
    private final BigDecimal flatRate;

    public FlatFeeStrategy(BigDecimal flatRate) {
        this.flatRate = flatRate;
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal computeFee(long hoursParked) {
        return flatRate;
    }
}
```

### How does ParkingLot orchestrate entry and exit?

The `ParkingLot` ties everything together.

```java
package com.bank.parking.service;

import com.bank.parking.model.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.UUID;

public class ParkingLot {
    private final List<ParkingFloor> floors;
    private final FeeStrategy feeStrategy;

    public ParkingLot(List<ParkingFloor> floors,
                      FeeStrategy feeStrategy) {
        this.floors = floors;
        this.feeStrategy = feeStrategy;
    }

    public Ticket entry(Vehicle vehicle) {
        for (ParkingFloor floor : floors) {
            var spot = floor.findNearestSpot(vehicle.getType());
            if (spot.isPresent()) {
                spot.get().occupy();
                Ticket ticket = new Ticket(
                        UUID.randomUUID().toString(),
                        vehicle, spot.get(), LocalDateTime.now());
                System.out.println("Assigned spot "
                        + spot.get().getSpotId()
                        + " on floor " + floor.getFloorId()
                        + " to " + vehicle.getLicensePlate());
                return ticket;
            }
        }
        throw new IllegalStateException("No spot available for "
                + vehicle.getType());
    }

    public BigDecimal exit(Ticket ticket) {
        ticket.getSpot().free();
        long hours = Duration.between(
                ticket.getEntryTime(), LocalDateTime.now()).toHours();
        BigDecimal fee = feeStrategy.computeFee(hours);
        System.out.println("Vehicle " + ticket.getVehicle()
                .getLicensePlate() + " exited, fee: " + fee);
        return fee;
    }
}
```

### How do you handle concurrency?

Two cars arriving at the same instant must never be assigned the same spot. The `ParkingSpot.occupy()` method is synchronised. In production, use an atomic compare-and-set on the occupied flag or a database-level lock.

```java
// Thread-safe spot allocation
public synchronized void occupy() {
    if (occupied) throw new IllegalStateException("Already occupied");
    occupied = true;
}
```

### What about extensibility?

The inevitable follow-up is "how would you add electric-vehicle charging spots?" A good design answers this by adding a new `VehicleType` (EV) and a spot attribute for charging capability, not by rewriting existing classes. The `FeeStrategy` can add a surcharge for charging, again as a new strategy class.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Replace linear spot scans with per-size priority queues for O(log n) allocation at scale.
- Cache free-spot counts per floor in Redis for display boards.
- Use the Strategy pattern for fees so pricing changes are configuration, not code changes.
- Model entry and exit as separate gate services in a distributed system, with the ticket as the shared state.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How would you add electric-vehicle charging spots?
- How would you implement surge pricing during peak hours?
- How do you prevent two vehicles from getting the same spot?
- What is the time complexity of finding the nearest spot?
- How would you scale this to a multi-building campus?
